['Here's a simple C code that uses `atoi()` function and takes command line arguments into consideration.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("No arguments provided.\n");
        return -1;
    } else {
        for (int i = 0; i < argc; ++i) {
            int converted = atoi(argv[i]); // Convert string to integer.

            if (converted == 0 && *argv[i] != '0') { 
                printf("Conversion error for '%s'\n", argv[i]);
            } else {
                printf("The converted value of '%s' is %d\n", argv[i], converted);
            }
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```
Please note that `atoi()` returns zero if it fails to convert a string into an integer. Also, the program assumes that you want to print out conversion errors only for non-zero strings (since `atoi("0")` would be fine but `atoi("foo")` is not). If you wish to treat "0" as a valid number, just remove the condition `&& *argv[i] != '0'`.
']

['Here is a C code that achieves what you asked for:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s index\n", argv[0]);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    int index = atoi(argv[1]); // convert the argument to an integer
    if ((index < 1) || (index >= argc)) {
        printf("Invalid index. It must be between 1 and %d\n", argc - 1);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // print the argument at the specified index
    printf("%s\n", argv[index]);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```
This program first checks that there is a command-line argument present. If not, it prints an error message and exits. 

The user's desired index is then read from the command line (the second argument) using `atoi` to convert the string to an integer. The program then checks whether this index is valid - i.e., between 1 and argc-1 inclusive, since argv[0] holds the name of the program itself and argv[argc] will be NULL (since there are only argc-1 arguments after the program's name). If it isn't, an error message is printed and the program exits.

Finally, if everything checks out, the program prints the argument at the specified index on a new line. 

The C standard requires that `argv[argc]` be NULL, so this code will work correctly even when no arguments are supplied after the program's name. If you want to print all command-line arguments, not just one of them, you would need to write a loop which prints each string in argv from index 1 onwards.
']

