['In order to generate a random number in C, we can use the rand() function from the stdlib library. However, it's important to remember that the rand() function will produce the same sequence of "random" numbers every time you run your program because it uses a constant seed by default. For generating truly random numbers, you have to seed your generator with something that changes, like the current time.

Here is an example:

```C
#include <stdlib.h> // for rand() and srand()
#include <time.h>   // for time()
#include <stdio.h>  // for printf()

int main(void) {
    /* Seed the random number generator with the current time */
    srand((unsigned int) time(NULL));

    /* Generate two random integers between 10 and 1000 */
    int num1 = rand() % (1000 - 10 + 1) + 10; // rand() generates a number from 0 to RAND_MAX
    int num2 = rand() % (1000 - 10 + 1) + 10;

    /* Calculate the difference between the two integers */
    int diff = abs(num1 - num2);

    printf("The difference is: %d\n", diff);

    return 0;
}
```

This code will print a random number between 10 and 1000 on each run. The `abs` function in C returns the absolute value of an integer, so we're sure that the difference is always positive, even if num1 < num2.
']

