['Sure, here's a simple program that allocates memory and then deallocates it in a loop. Note that I'm using `calloc()` to allocate memory because it initializes the allocated memory to zero, which is helpful for debugging as it can tell us if we are still accessing freed memory after it has been freed.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    size_t buffer_size = 1024;  // Size of the buffer in bytes
    int* buffer = NULL;        // Pointer to the allocated memory

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        printf("Allocating buffer %d...\n", i+1);

        // Allocate memory
        buffer = (int*)calloc(buffer_size, 1);
        if (buffer == NULL) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory!\n");
            return EXIT_FAILURE;
        }

        // Use the allocated memory...
        // ...

        printf("Deallocating buffer %d...\n", i+1);

        // Deallocate memory
        free(buffer);
        buffer = NULL;
    }

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```
Please replace the `// Use the allocated memory...` comment with your actual usage of this memory. In a real-world program, you would want to use it for something meaningful like storing data or executing some computations. 

Also, remember that every time we call `calloc()` to allocate new memory, we should also check if the allocation was successful (i.e., `buffer == NULL`). If an allocation fails, you'll want to handle it gracefully (for instance, by printing a message to stderr and exiting the program with `EXIT_FAILURE`).
