['Here's a simple C program that uses `scanf` to read a string from standard input, up until it reaches a newline character or has read 1023 characters. It also keeps track of the number of characters read in and does not store the newline character.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char buffer[1024]; // Buffer to hold input string
    int count = 0; // Counter for characters read

    while (count < 1023) { // Loop until 1023 characters have been read or a newline has been encountered
        char c = getchar(); // Read next character

        if (c == '\n' || c == EOF) { // If we've reached a newline or end of file, break the loop
            break;
        } else {
            buffer[count++] = c; // Store the character and increment counter
        }
    }

    buffer[count] = '\0'; // Null-terminate the string

    printf("Read in: %s\n", buffer); // Print out the result
    printf("Counted %d characters.\n", count); // Print out how many characters were read

    return 0;
}
```

Please note that this code will not behave correctly if a string is entered that is longer than 1023 characters (it does not handle the case where more than 1023 characters are entered). Also, it assumes that input is being typed in interactively. If you're redirecting input from a file or another source, additional handling may be necessary.
']

