    if (file != NULL) {
        while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file) != NULL) {
            printf("%s", buffer);
        }

        pclose(file);
    } else {
        perror("Failed to execute command");
    }
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {

    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: ./program username\n");
        return 1;
    }

    char command[50];
    sprintf(command, "cat /etc/passwd | grep %s", argv[1]);

    executeCommand(command);

    return 0;
}
```

This code first checks if a username was provided as a command line argument. If not, it prints out the usage and terminates the program. Then, it prepares the shell command to be executed by constructing the grep command with the given username and then invoking the `executeCommand` function passing this constructed command as an argument.
The `executeCommand` function opens a pipe using `popen` for executing commands in shell and reads from this pipe line by line, printing each line on standard output (console). The `pclose` is used to close the pipe after reading all data. If opening the pipe fails, it prints an error message.
